bonfyre books — book bank and card lines to your accounts, on this machine

Usage:
  bonfyre books learn --history categorised.csv --out learned.json
  bonfyre books --transactions bank.csv --learned learned.json [options]
  bonfyre books --transactions bank.csv --chart chart.txt [options]
  bonfyre books inspect --workbook bank.xlsx
  bonfyre books review --report report.json --out review.xlsx
  bonfyre books --transactions bank.xlsx --sheet Transactions --chart chart.txt --out review.xlsx

learn reads lines you have already categorised (a Category or Account
column) and keeps what it learned in one file. Booking then answers from that
file first; a line it is unsure of goes to the local model, which is shown
your most similar earlier lines. Without learned books every line goes to the
local model and is held for your review, and a line the local model
suggests is held for review too. An amount that does not read as money is
kept as written and the line held; it is never booked as zero. Nothing
leaves this machine.

Options:
  --transactions FILE  Bank or card export with a description column (date and amount if present)
  --learned FILE       What bonfyre books learn wrote
  --chart FILE         One account per line, "Name: what goes there" (19 at most)
  --columns MAP        Which header is which, e.g. date=Posted,description=Payee,amount=Amount
                       (needed when the file could be read two ways)
  --sheet NAME         Exact XLSX sheet name (required when there is more than one)
  --header-row N       XLSX header row, numbered from 1 (defaults to the first used row)
  --out FILE           Review workbook (.xlsx), full report (.json), or categorized CSV
  --json               Print the full report
  --model NAME         Prefer this local model (a file name or part of one);
                       otherwise the front loads the largest that fits
  --model-endpoint U   Local model server (default http://127.0.0.1:7000)

If the transactions file has an Account or Category column, the summary says
how many lines matched it.

Example:
  bonfyre books learn --history examples/history.csv --out learned.json
  bonfyre books --transactions examples/september.csv --learned learned.json --out booked.csv
